Ten useful features and techniques









1. Start with a consistent template

Free/basic design workflow

Choose one template and apply it consistently instead of designing every slide independently.

Demonstration: create a title slide, a concept slide, and a conclusion slide using the same style.












2. Establish visual hierarchy

Basic design technique

Use a clear title, one main idea, and readable supporting text.

Demonstration: replace a paragraph-heavy slide with a headline and three concise points.












3. Align elements precisely

Basic editor tools; interface labels may vary

Select multiple elements and use the available alignment, positioning, or spacing controls.

Demonstration: line up three concept cards and make their gaps consistent.












4. Use diagrams for processes

Basic shapes and connectors

Use shapes and arrows to communicate how a process works.

Demonstration: draw a neural network pipeline from input to prediction.












5. Use charts for comparisons

Basic chart options; advanced features may vary

Turn numerical comparisons into charts, keeping labels, units, and the source visible.

Demonstration: compare two clearly labelled fictional model results. Do not present invented data as research.












6. Group and layer elements

Use grouping and layer controls to keep related text, icons, and shapes together and prevent accidental overlap.












7. Use restrained animation

Animate an important process or use a subtle transition. Avoid applying different effects to every element.












8. Prepare speaker notes

Add explanations to presenter notes where supported so the slide stays concise while you retain the detail needed to speak confidently.












9. Collaborate and share

Use available sharing permissions and collaboration controls to collect feedback without exchanging multiple conflicting copies.












10. Export and inspect

Export to an appropriate supported format, including PowerPoint where available, and inspect the exported file for missing fonts, shifted elements, and changed animations.

Final slide-quality checklist
- Typography: no more than two main font families.
- Contrast: text remains readable against the background.
- Alignment: objects follow a consistent grid.
- Text density: one central message per slide.
- Hierarchy: the viewer can identify the key point quickly.
- Image quality: avoid blurry or irrelevant visuals.
- Animation: use movement only when it helps comprehension.
- Consistency: reuse colors, spacing, and heading styles.
- Sources: cite external facts and images appropriately.
- Export: inspect the final file before presenting.